event:message
data:{"id":"407622e7-1382-4efe-82e7-64344b8125a0","response_type":"tool_result","content":"","done":false,"data":{"ambiguous_slugs":{},"duration_ms":5,"error":"","found_kbs":{"index":["fed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581"]},"output":"\u003cwiki_page\u003e\n\u003cmetadata\u003e\n\u003cknowledge_base_id\u003efed4ec72-0043-49ac-b350-19a4c5a3a581\u003c/knowledge_base_id\u003e\n\u003clink\u003e[[index|Index]]\u003c/link\u003e\n\u003ctype\u003eindex\u003c/type\u003e\n\u003caliases\u003e\u003c/aliases\u003e\n\u003c/metadata\u003e\n\u003crelationships\u003e\n\u003clinks_to\u003e(none)\u003c/links_to\u003e\n\u003clinked_from\u003e(none)\u003c/linked_from\u003e\n\u003c/relationships\u003e\n\u003csources\u003e\n\n\u003c/sources\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003e\n# Wiki 索引\n\n本页面为自动更新的索引页，随新文档的添加而动态同步。近期新增内容涵盖了 DOS、Mac OS、UNIX 及 Linux 等主流操作系统的概述与对比，显著丰富了本站在计算平台领域的知识范围。\n\u003c/summary\u003e\n\u003ccontent\u003e\n# Wiki 索引\n\n本页面为自动更新的索引页，随新文档的添加而动态同步。近期新增内容涵盖了 DOS、Mac OS、UNIX 及 Linux 等主流操作系统的概述与对比，显著丰富了本站在计算平台领域的知识范围。\n\n## Summary (4)\n\n[[summary/95bc02d7-d130-4725-8e15-97455995ba58|native-passage-1 - Summary]] — This document outlines Mac OS, the official operating system for Apple Macintosh computers, highlighting its graphical user interface and consistent application design.\n[[summary/ee9f60e3-062c-49cb-bad4-d8ccd3e8083c|native-passage-2 - Summary]] — This document defines UNIX as a versatile multi-tasking command-line and network operating system, while introducing Linux as a popular, free UNIX-type alternative for various computing environments.\n[[summary/d4fdffd0-5c7d-4290-adbb-f5e1f7ae80b0|native-passage-3 - Summary]] — This document compares popular non-Windows operating systems, highlighting Linux distributions like Ubuntu and Mint, and noting the similarities between FreeBSD and Linux despite kernel differences.\n[[summary/9286e345-411e-42bf-8083-02c97dac28b2|native-passage-4 - Summary]] — This document provides a brief overview of DOS, an early personal computer operating system characterized by its command-line interface where users interact via text commands at a prompt.\n\n## Entity (10)\n\n[[entity/chrome-os|Chrome OS]] — Chrome OS is an operating system mentioned as an alternative to Windows for personal computers, listed alongside Linux and FreeBSD in discussions of non-Windows computing platforms.\n[[entity/dos|DOS]] — DOS is an early personal computer operating system that uses a text-based command-line interface, requiring users to type commands at a prompt like c:\\\u003e to manage files and run applications.\n[[entity/freebsd|FreeBSD]] — FreeBSD is an open-source operating system that uses a different kernel than Linux but shares much of the same software ecosystem, offering a similar desktop experience.\n[[entity/linux|Linux]] — Linux is a popular, free, multi-tasking Unix-like operating system recommended for PC users seeking alternatives to Windows, with Ubuntu and Linux Mint being notable distributions.\n[[entity/linux-mint|Linux Mint]] — Linux Mint is a popular Linux distribution recommended for personal computers as a user-friendly, stable, and secure alternative to proprietary operating systems.\n[[entity/mac-os|Mac OS]] — Mac OS is the official operating system for Apple Macintosh computers, featuring a graphical user interface with consistent visual elements across all applications.\n[[entity/ubuntu|Ubuntu]] — Ubuntu is a popular Linux distribution recommended for users seeking a non-Windows operating system for daily use on personal computers.\n[[entity/unix|UNIX]] — UNIX is a multi-tasking, command-line operating system that functions as both an independent and network OS across various hardware architectures.\n[[entity/macintosh|Macintosh]] — Macintosh is a line of personal computers designed and sold by Apple, serving as the hardware platform for the Mac OS operating system with a consistent graphical user interface.\n[[entity/apple|Apple]] — Apple is the technology company that develops and owns the Macintosh computer line and its associated Mac OS operating system.\n\n## Concept (6)\n\n[[concept/multi-tasking-operating-system|Multi-tasking Operating System]] — A multi-tasking operating system enables the concurrent execution of multiple processes or tasks, a capability found in systems like UNIX and Linux.\n[[concept/network-operating-system|Network Operating System]] — A Network Operating System supports workstations, personal computers, and terminals on a local area network. UNIX is cited as an example that functions as both an independent and network operating system.\n[[concept/open-source-operating-system|Open-source operating system]] — This page defines open-source operating systems as platforms with publicly accessible source code, highlighting Linux distributions like Ubuntu and Mint, as well as FreeBSD.\n[[concept/unix-like-operating-system|Unix-like operating system]] — A Unix-like operating system is a category of systems behaving similarly to Unix, including Linux and FreeBSD, which share similar software ecosystems despite kernel differences.\n[[concept/graphical-user-interface|Graphical User Interface]] — A visual interface allowing users to interact with electronic devices through graphical icons and visual indicators, contrasting with text-based command-line systems.\n[[concept/command-line-interface|命令行界面]] — 命令行界面是一种基于文本的用户交互方式，用户通过在提示符下输入特定命令来执行程序和管理文件，区别于图形用户界面。\n\n_To explore more pages under any category, use wiki_search with a query, or read a specific slug directly._\n\n\u003c/content\u003e\n\u003c/wiki_page\u003e","success":true,"tool_call_id":"call_f3b5e381b81f422dbdf0588c","tool_name":"wiki_read_page"}}

